## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of temporal, multi-step constraints in a 3-valued logic setting. Requires constructing a concrete model and outlining a backtracking propagator that preserves non-F constraints while allowing Unknowns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued semantics for implication
- Temporal constraint propagation across time steps
- Unit/branching propagation in backtracking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: evaluateImp(A,B) returns Kleene(A,B)
function evaluateImp(A,B){ // A,B in {T,F,U}
  // ¬A ∨ B with Kleene ordering T>U>F
  const not = {T:'F', F:'T', U:'U'}[A];
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  // ⩒ is max by value order
  const values = [not, B];
  let best = 'F';
  for (const v of values) if (order[v] > order[best]) best = v;
  return best;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a small CSP with Kleene semantics, reason about propagation, and craft a concrete counterfactual assignment with U, plus depth-limited reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬ maps T↔F, U↔U.
- Implication as ¬X ∨ Y; F only when antecedent is T and consequent F.
- Depth-limited backtracking with constraint propagation to avoid exploring futile branches.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function kleeneOr(a,b){ return a==='T'||b==='T'?'T': a==='U'||b==='U'?'U':'F';}
function kleeneImp(a,b){ // a -> b
  if(a==='T' && b==='F') return 'F';
  if(a==='T' && b==='U') return 'U';
  if(a==='F') return 'T';
  if(a==='U' && b==='F') return 'U';
  return (b==='T') ? 'T' : 'U';
}
```

